## ***********************************************************************
## **
## **  GPB.INC.BL -- the GP.BASIC keyword definitions for BASLOAD.
## **
## **  #INCLUDE "GPB.INC.BL" at the top of any BASL source that uses GP
## **  keywords. BASLOAD only knows the ROM's keywords; this teaches it
## **  the GP tokens so GP.DO parses instead of raising a syntax error.
## **  The host-side tokeniser (bin/tokenise.zip) does not need this file
## **  -- it learns the same tokens from c64tokens.py at build time.
## **
## **  THE TOKEN VALUES ARE THE ABI. They mirror getGP() in
## **  source/common-scripts/c64tokens.py exactly: $CE7F down, one per
## **  keyword, allocated DOWNWARD and never renumbered. When a keyword
## **  is added there, add it here in the same change.
## **
## **  A PRG containing these tokens is compile-only: the ROM cannot
## **  LIST or RUN it, because nothing in BASIC is behind $CE7x.
## **
## ***********************************************************************
#IFNDEF GP.DEFS
#DEFINE GP.DEFS 1

## Loops -- GP.DO <count> ... GP.LOOP, count 0 or omitted = forever.
## GP.EXITDO leaves the innermost GP.DO early, closing its frame, so it
## is the leak-free exit; jumping out with GOTO abandons the frame.

#TOKEN GP.DO 52863
#TOKEN GP.LOOP 52862
#TOKEN GP.EXITDO 52861

## Machine code. GP.CALL sets the registers from its arguments (all optional, all
## defaulting to 0) and the four value words read the results back, out of SYS's own
## $030C-$030F -- so they also read what a plain SYS left behind.
##
## WARNING: PUT THE CODE IN BANKED RAM ($A000-$BFFF), NOT $0400-$07FF. Stock BASIC
## leaves $0400 free for the user; a COMPILED program keeps runtime state there, and
## POKEing code over it corrupts the program silently.

#TOKEN GP.CALL 52860
#TOKEN GP.A 52859
#TOKEN GP.X 52858
#TOKEN GP.Y 52857
#TOKEN GP.C 52856

## Strings. GP.INSTR is the search GPC otherwise has no form of at all. GP.STRPTR
## returns the address of [ActLen][Data] -- length AT that address, first character at
## +1, capacity at -2 -- so machine code can fill a BASIC string in place and set its
## length, which stock BASIC cannot do.
##
## Splitting that address, do NOT use "P AND 255": AND is 16-bit SIGNED and the string
## heap lives above 32767, so it raises OUT OF RANGE. Use GP.LOBYTE / GP.HIBYTE.

#TOKEN GP.INSTR 52855
#TOKEN GP.STRPTR 52854


## GP.TRIM, GP.LTRIM, GP.RTRIM, GP.UPPER and GP.LOWER ARE GONE -- STRCASE.INC.BL now,
## in GP.ASM, built on GP.STRPTR above: it takes the block address and rewrites it
## where it lies, no copy either way.
##
##      #SYMFILE "@:MYPROG.SYM"          <- and it is not optional
##      #INCLUDE "STRCASE.INC.BL"
##      STRCASE.PTR = GP.STRPTR(LINE$)
##      STRCASE.MODE = STRCASE.RTRIM
##      GOSUB STRCASE.GO
##
## ONE THING WAS LOST: the compiler used to reject a literal at the call site.
## GP.STRPTR("text") is an address inside the p-code, and upper-casing it edits the
## program. And there is still NO GP.PAD, for the reason there never was one -- padding
## GROWS a string, and nothing working on the block alone can pass the capacity it was
## born with. Use STRHELP.PAD.

## GP.COMP(a$,b$) compares WITHOUT case: -1 if a$ sorts before b$, 0 if they
## match, 1 if after. Length breaks a tie, so "abc" sorts before "ABCD".
## Use it for a case-blind equality test -- IF GP.COMP(A$,B$) = 0 -- which
## plain "=" cannot do, and as the comparator when ordering names.

#TOKEN GP.COMP 52848

## GP.SORT IS GONE -- SORT.INC.BL, the same shell sort with the same Ciura gaps, in
## GP.ASM. Built on GP.ARRPTR below, which stays: a BASL subroutine cannot be handed an
## array, so something has to turn one into an address.
##
##      #SYMFILE "@:MYPROG.SYM"          <- and it is not optional
##      #INCLUDE "SORT.INC.BL"
##      SORT.PTR = GP.ARRPTR(NAME$())
##      SORT.DESCEND = 0 : SORT.NOCASE = 0
##      GOSUB SORT.RUN

## GP.ARRPTR(a()) -- the address of element ZERO, header already skipped, so machine
## code can work on an array in bulk. STRIDE IS YOURS TO ADD: 2 bytes an element for a
## string array (each element POINTS at the string's block), 6 for a numeric one.
## Multi-dimensional arrays are rejected.
##
## Empty parentheses required -- GP.ARRPTR(A(3)) is a syntax error, add 3*2 or 3*6
## yourself. Split the address with GP.LOBYTE / GP.HIBYTE, never P AND 255.

#TOKEN GP.ARRPTR 52846

## GP.STASH AND GP.RESTR ARE GONE -- STASH.INC.BL, in GP.ASM, doing the same job: a
## self-describing header, restore-anywhere, the 4,094-cell bank limit. Tokens 52845
## and 52844 are retired, not reused.

## Text drawing, written STRAIGHT INTO VERA -- no KERNAL, which is why it is several
## times faster than PRINT, whose every character is two KERNAL calls carrying scroll,
## quote mode and cursor work.
##
##   GP.BOX x,y,w,h [,style] [,col]     frame only, styles 0-3:
##                                      0 solid  1 single line
##                                      2 rounded  3 thick
##                                      ...or an ADDRESS, see below
##   GP.FILL x,y,w,h,char [,col]        char is PETSCII, e.g. ASC(" ")
##   GP.CHAR x,y,char [,col]            ONE cell -- GP.FILL of a 1x1 box
##   GP.PRINTAT x,y,text$ [,col]
##
## THE COLOUR IS OPTIONAL and defaults to what COLOR last set -- the colour a PRINT
## would have used. Supplied, it is background * 16 + foreground, as the X16 packs it.
## 255 is a REAL colour (light grey on light grey), not a "leave it alone" marker.
##
## GP.BOX's two optionals cannot be skipped over: GP.BOX X,Y,W,H,,7 is a syntax error,
## GP.BOX X,Y,W,H,0,7 is what you meant.
##
## A STYLE OF 256 OR MORE IS AN ADDRESS -- your own eight glyphs, in the order top
## right, top left, bottom right, bottom left, top edge, bottom edge, left, right.
##
##   B$ = CHR$(110)+CHR$(112)+CHR$(125)+CHR$(109)+CHR$(64)+CHR$(64)+CHR$(66)+CHR$(66)
##   GP.BOX 4, 2, 30, 8, GP.STRPTR(B$) + 1, COL
##
## NOTE THE + 1: GP.STRPTR points at the LENGTH byte and the glyphs are the data. Leave
## it off and the box is drawn with the string length as its top-right corner.
##
## THEY ARE SCREEN CODES, NOT PETSCII, and that differs from GP.FILL on purpose: the
## built-in table goes straight to the cell unconverted, so a custom set has to be in
## the same alphabet. GP.FILL converts what you hand it; this does not.
##
## WHICH IS ALSO HOW TO DRAW A BOX IN ISO MODE, where the built-in styles come out as
## letters and there is nothing to translate to -- ISO-8859-15 has no box-drawing
## characters. But a tile index IS a character code there, so hand it ASCII:
##
##     ISO.GLYPH$ = "++++--||"
##     GP.BOX 50, 26, 4, 3, GP.STRPTR(ISO.GLYPH$) + 1, 1
##
## See ISO.EXP.BL, which reads the cells back and asserts 43, 45, 124. A program with a
## RE-ORDERED FONT needs the same escape, samples/editor being the one in this tree.
##
## WARNING -- THESE DO NOT MOVE THE CURSOR PRINT USES. Nothing here calls the KERNAL, so
## a PRINT after a GP.PRINTAT carries on from wherever the KERNAL still thinks the cursor
## is. Use LOCATE first, or stay in one world or the other.
##
## Nothing is clipped: off the right edge wraps, off the bottom writes past the end of
## the map. Zero width or height draws nothing, which is the case a computed size
## reaches by accident.

## GP.CHAR IS NOT A NEW COMMAND -- it is GP.FILL with the 1s written for you. The
## compiler pushes them and emits GP.FILL's own token, so the runtime block does not
## grow by a byte.
##
##   GP.FILL X, Y, 1, 1, ED.GL.TL, C      ->   GP.CHAR X, Y, ED.GL.TL, C
##
## The char is a NUMBER, as GP.FILL's is, not a one-character string.
##
#TOKEN GP.BOX 52843
#TOKEN GP.FILL 52842
#TOKEN GP.CHAR 52824
#TOKEN GP.PRINTAT 52841

## GP.MENU AND GP.SEL ARE GONE -- MENUVERT.INC.BL, written in ordinary GPB commands and
## doing the same job: cursor keys, RETURN, ESC, case-blind hotkeys that choose their
## row, must-select / keep-mark / no-wrap, and the SNES pad. For no runtime bytes.
##
##      #INCLUDE "MENUVERT.INC.BL"
##      MENUVERT.X = 8 : MENUVERT.Y = 6
##      MENUVERT.WIDTH = 24 : MENUVERT.COUNT = 4
##      MENUVERT.ITEM$(1) = " NEW GAME"        ... and so on
##      MENUVERT.ATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.TEXT)
##      MENUVERT.HIATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.HILITE)
##      MENUVERT.HOT$ = "NLOQ"
##      GOSUB MENUVERT.RUN                     answer in MENUVERT.SEL
##
## The one behaviour that did NOT survive is the colourless highlight: the assembly
## swapped the cell's attribute nibbles, the module prints the row again and so wants a
## colour. MENUVERT.HIATTR = 0 asks for that inversion back.

##
## GP.SELECT <var>             a plain numeric variable, re-read at each alternative
## GP.CASE <expr> [,<expr>..]  run the body below if any of them matches
## GP.OTHER                    run the body below if nothing above matched
## GP.ENDSEL                   close the select -- REQUIRED, it ends the block
##
##   GP.SELECT K
##   GP.CASE 13
##     PRINT "RETURN"
##   GP.CASE 17, 145
##     PRINT "CURSOR"
##   GP.OTHER
##     PRINT "SOMETHING ELSE"
##   GP.ENDSEL
##
## NOT a replacement for ON x GOTO/GOSUB, which is a real skip table and still right for
## a dense 1..n index. This is for the SPARSE selector -- key codes, state machines.
##
## Case values are ordinary numeric expressions, compared in the order written; the
## first match wins. Nothing matching with no GP.OTHER is not an error. Selects nest.
##
## THE SELECTOR IS STRICTER: a plain numeric variable, not an expression, a constant or
## an array element -- write those to a scalar first. In return the whole construct
## compiles to core opcodes, so a program whose only GP keyword is a select does not
## carry the GP runtime block at all, exactly like GP.IF.
##
## A case body may share the line after a colon, which is what makes a sparse dispatch
## read as a table:
##
##   GP.CASE 157 : GOSUB MOVE.LEFT
##   GP.CASE 29  : GOSUB MOVE.RIGHT
##
## GOTO out of a select is safe -- it keeps nothing alive. GP.DO does open a frame, and
## a GOTO leaving one is covered: the compiler puts an .unwind in front of it and
## FixBranches fills in the count, for no runtime bytes.

#TOKEN GP.SELECT 52838
#TOKEN GP.CASE 52837
#TOKEN GP.OTHER 52836
#TOKEN GP.ENDSEL 52835

##
## GP.IF <expr> THEN         run the body below if the condition is true
## GP.ELSEIF <expr> THEN     ... otherwise try this one
## GP.ELSE                   ... otherwise run this one
## GP.ENDIF                  close the IF -- REQUIRED
##
##   GP.IF N < 0 THEN
##     PRINT "NEGATIVE"
##   GP.ELSEIF N = 0 THEN
##     PRINT "ZERO"
##   GP.ELSE
##     PRINT "POSITIVE"
##   GP.ENDIF
##
## A BLOCK if, and the block form is the only form: each keyword ALONE ON ITS LINE, THEN
## required with nothing after it. GP.IF X > 5 THEN PRINT is a syntax error -- stock
## IF ... THEN is untouched and still there for that.
##
## GP.ELSEIF may repeat, GP.ELSE is optional, nothing matching is not an error. IFs nest,
## including inside a GP.SELECT case body or a GP.DO loop.
##
## No frame to release, so a GOTO out is safe; GP.ENDIF only marks where the block ends.
## 12 bytes of runtime for the whole construct, because all four opcodes reuse handlers
## that were already there.

#TOKEN GP.IF 52830
#TOKEN GP.ELSEIF 52829
#TOKEN GP.ELSE 52828
#TOKEN GP.ENDIF 52827

## GP.ASM / GP.ENDASM -- inline 65C02 assembly, assembled at compile time.
##
##   #REM 1
##   GP.ASM
##   REM lda #72
##   REM jsr $ffd2
##   REM ldx #5
##   REM loop: dex
##   REM bne loop
##   GP.ENDASM
##   #REM 0
##
## The body rides in REM statements, one instruction a line, because BASLOAD stores REM
## text BYTE FOR BYTE -- ORA, AND, EOR and ROR survive there and are tokenised into
## keywords anywhere else. Lower case is upshifted for you.
##
## #REM 1 IS REQUIRED (BASLOAD's default is 0, which strips the body before the compiler
## sees it). The two delimiters are real keywords rather than REMs, so an empty block is
## a named error -- BLOCK MISMATCH -- and not a program silently containing no code.
## Turn REMs back off after GP.ENDASM.
##
## COSTS NOTHING AT RUN TIME: five bytes of p-code plus the instructions, reusing
## handlers every program already has. A program whose only GP.BASIC keyword is GP.ASM
## does NOT pull in the 2K GP runtime block.
##
## LABELS. name: on its own line or in front of an instruction, six characters
## significant, and belonging to their block -- the same name in two blocks is two
## labels. A branch further than 127 bytes is OUT OF RANGE at COMPILE time, on its line.
##
## {VAR} is the ADDRESS OF A BASIC VARIABLE'S SLOT -- so LDA {N%} reads
## it and STA {N%} writes it, in the slot BASIC itself uses:
##
##   {N}  {N%}  {N$}   the scalar's slot
##   {N()}             the ARRAY's slot, which holds the base address of
##                     its data -- an element is two steps, as it is
##                     through GP.ARRPTR
##
## >> {VAR} NEEDS #SYMFILE. BASLOAD renames variables -- N% becomes A% -- and does not
## >> rename REM text with them, so the name in the braces is not the name the code
## >> uses. #SYMFILE is BASLOAD's record of that mapping, and the compiler reads it:
## >>
## >>   #SYMFILE "@:PROG.SYM"        named to match the PRG -- PROG.PRG reads PROG.SYM
## >>
## >> Miss it and the compile stops with NO SYMBOL FILE FOR {}. And {VAR} never CREATES
## >> a variable, so assign it in BASIC first, even M% = 0, or you get UNKNOWN VARIABLE
## >> IN {}. See ASM.EXP.BL.
##
## NO EXPRESSIONS: {N%}+1 and LABEL+2 are not understood -- use an index register. Zero
## page or absolute is chosen by the OPERAND ($34 against $0034), and every 65C02 mode
## is available except BBR/BBS/RMB/SMB.
##
## Bodies may also come from a file, which is the same thing spelt
## differently -- #INCLUDE splices it in verbatim:
##
##   #REM 1
##   GP.ASM
##   #INCLUDE "MACPTR.ASM"
##   GP.ENDASM
##   #REM 0
#TOKEN GP.ASM 52826
#TOKEN GP.ENDASM 52825

## GP.CONTAINS(a$,b$) is -1 if b$ occurs anywhere in a$, 0 if not. CASE SENSITIVE. Use
## GP.INSTR for the POSITION instead of a flag, GP.COMP for a case-blind test.
##
## An EMPTY needle is 0, not -1 -- GP.INSTR reports "not found" for one, and this is
## built on it. COMPOSITE: the compiler expands it to GP.INSTR(a$,b$)<>0, so the object
## is identical to writing that by hand and the keyword costs nothing to have.

#TOKEN GP.CONTAINS 52834

## GP.HIBYTE(n) / GP.LOBYTE(n) split an address into the two bytes a 6502 takes.
## Everything that eats an address a byte at a time wants them -- GP.CALL's registers,
## VERA's $9F20/$9F21.
##
##   P = GP.STRPTR(A$)
##   GP.CALL $A000, GP.LOBYTE(P), GP.HIBYTE(P)
##
## USE THESE, NOT "N AND 255", which is the spelling that looks right and is a live bug:
## AND is 16-bit SIGNED and these addresses are above 32767, so it raises OUT OF RANGE
## instead of masking. Range 0 to 65535, and COMPOSITE -- no runtime code at all.

#TOKEN GP.HIBYTE 52833
#TOKEN GP.LOBYTE 52832

## GP.ISEMPTY(a$) is -1 when a$ has zero length, 0 otherwise. A string of SPACES is not
## empty -- STRCASE.TRIM it first if that is what you meant.
##
## For READABILITY and nothing else: it is the same four bytes as LEN(A$)=0, and IF
## A$="" costs about the same, so do not choose between the three on size.

#TOKEN GP.ISEMPTY 52831

#ENDIF
